ns prologos::ocapn::protocols :no-prelude

;; ========================================
;; OCapN protocol session types — Phase 53
;; ========================================
;;
;; The OCapN spec defines several stateful message exchanges between
;; peers; this module captures each as a session type. These serve
;; three roles:
;;
;;   1. SPECIFICATION — the canonical statement of what messages
;;      flow in what order between peers. The bridge's handler
;;      code (`captp-incoming-with-state`, `pump-outbound`, etc.)
;;      should be reviewable against these protocols: each handler
;;      branch corresponds to a session step.
;;
;;   2. DUALITY CHECK — every session type has a dual; the
;;      `test-ocapn-protocols.rkt` test suite verifies dual-of-dual
;;      is identity (the dual round-trip), confirming the
;;      protocols are well-formed.
;;
;;   3. FUTURE RUNTIME WIRING — a later phase can replace ad-hoc
;;      BridgeState dispatch with session-typed channels driven by
;;      the propagator network (cf. `io-bridge.rkt` /
;;      `session-runtime.rkt`'s pattern for FileRead/FileWrite).
;;
;; Each session here is written from ONE peer's perspective —
;; typically the LOCAL peer. The remote peer follows the DUAL.

;; ----------------------------------------
;; Payload types
;; ----------------------------------------
;;
;; All op-level messages are SyrupValue on the wire. Concrete
;; semantic types (CapTPOp, Refr, etc.) live in
;; `prologos::ocapn::{message, refr}`. For session-type purposes
;; we use String — every encoded op fits in a String when serialised
;; (newline-framed). A future revision can refine the payload type
;; to `CapTPOp` once the cross-module session-elaboration story
;; supports user-defined data types in session positions.
;;
;; For now: think of `! String` as "send one op:* on the wire" and
;; `? String` as "receive one op:* from the wire."

;; ========================================
;; 1. Handshake (Phase 25)
;; ========================================
;;
;; Both peers MUST exchange op:start-session before any other op
;; flows. From OUR perspective: we send first, then receive peer's.
;; (In practice the bridge pre-queues OUR session in pending-out and
;; expects peer's to be the first inbound op; the handlers are
;; `bridge-state-with-our-session` + the `op-start-session` arm in
;; `captp-incoming-with-state`.)
session Handshake
  ! String
  ? String
  end

;; ========================================
;; 2. Question-Answer (Phase 25 questioner-side)
;; ========================================
;;
;; The atomic request/reply pair: we send `op:deliver` with an
;; answer-pos, peer eventually replies with
;; `op:deliver-to-answer`. Bridge handler:
;; `bridge-send-question` (emit) + `dispatch-incoming-answer`
;; (receive + resolve local promise).
;;
;; The reply MAY carry a broken-promise <Error _> as the payload;
;; from the protocol's perspective it's just "a String" — the
;; bridge interprets the payload's shape.
session QuestionAnswer
  ! String
  ? String
  end

;; ========================================
;; 3. Pipelined-question (Phase 38-49)
;; ========================================
;;
;; After we've sent a question, we may pipeline additional deliveries
;; targeting the question's answer-pos BEFORE the answer arrives.
;; Peer queues them on the local promise; on resolution, peer
;; forwards them to the resolved refr (Phase 41/44) or errors them
;; out (Phase 45/46). From OUR perspective the loop is internal
;; choice (we decide each turn whether to pipeline more or wait):
session PipelinedQuestion
  ! String
  rec
    +>
      | :pipeline -> ! String -> rec
      | :await -> ? String -> end

;; ========================================
;; 4. Listen (Phase 48)
;; ========================================
;;
;; Register a one-shot notification on a target promise; eventually
;; receive `op:deliver` to our resolver-pos when the promise
;; settles. Bridge handler: `bs-add-listener` + `listener-notify-loop`
;; in pump-outbound (or `bs-handle-listen-with-late-fire` for the
;; already-settled case — Phase 51).
session ListenProtocol
  ! String
  ? String
  end

;; ========================================
;; 5. GC export (Phase 34e / 50)
;; ========================================
;;
;; One-way notification: we dropped k references to peer's export N.
;; No reply expected; peer decrements their refcount.
;; Bridge handler: `release-import` (imperative) or
;; `connection-queue-release-import` (declarative, Phase 50).
session GcExport
  ! String
  end

;; ========================================
;; 6. Gift handoff — recipient-to-exporter (Phase 52+)
;; ========================================
;;
;; The OCapN three-vat handoff has multiple legs. This session is
;; from the RECIPIENT's perspective dialing the exporter: send
;; `op:withdraw-gift`, await the capability delivery.
;;
;; Phase 52 ships only the gift-table scaffolding
;; (`bs-add-gift`/`bs-lookup-gift`); op:deposit-gift and
;; op:withdraw-gift wire encoding + bridge dispatch + netlayer-tcp
;; outbound dialing are deferred to subsequent phases.
session GiftWithdraw
  ! String
  ? String
  end

;; ========================================
;; 7. Gift handoff — gifter-to-exporter (Phase 52+, deferred)
;; ========================================
;;
;; One-way: gifter notifies exporter "I deposited a gift for the
;; recipient at gift-id G."
session GiftDeposit
  ! String
  end

;; ========================================
;; 8. The whole CapTP session — responder view
;; ========================================
;;
;; From the RESPONDER's perspective: receive peer's session bytes,
;; respond with our own, then enter the main loop offering each
;; possible inbound op. The loop only ends on op:abort.
;;
;; This is the protocol against which `captp-incoming-with-state`'s
;; dispatch should be reviewed: every offered branch corresponds
;; to one of its match arms. The bridge HANDLES each op without
;; producing the analogous wire reply — for symmetry with the
;; CapTPOp constructors, we model the inbound side as `?` and
;; outbound responses as separate sessions (Question/Listen/etc.
;; cover the responses).
session CapTPSession
  ? String
  ! String
  rec
    &>
      | :deliver           -> ? String -> rec
      | :deliver-to-answer -> ? String -> rec
      | :deliver-only      -> ? String -> rec
      | :listen            -> ? String -> rec
      | :gc-export         -> ? String -> rec
      | :gc-answer         -> ? String -> rec
      | :abort             -> ? String -> end
